Thanks — based on the details in your memory (married filing jointly, W-2 $79,400; self‑employment/contracting $8,900; interest income $920 + $320; home office 100 sq ft exclusive in a 780 sq ft rental at $2,400/month; student loan interest $4,100; $1,400 charitable; $890 unreimbursed expenses), here’s a clear, practical summary of what you can likely deduct, whether to itemize, how the home office works, and what tax forms you’ll need.

What you can deduct (high level)
- Self‑employment business expenses: Your IT contracting income ($8,900) is reported on Schedule C. Ordinary and necessary business expenses directly related to that work are deductible against that income (equipment, software, supplies, phone/internet portion used for business, mileage, etc.). The $890 you listed — if those were business expenses for your contracting — can be deducted on Schedule C. If they were unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job, they are NOT deductible for federal tax (suspended since 2018).
- Home office: If the 100 sq ft is used exclusively and regularly for the contracting business, you can take a home‑office deduction on Schedule C. Two choices:
  - Simplified method: $5 × 100 sq ft = $500 deduction.
  - Actual method: business % = 100/780 ≈ 12.82%. Annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800 → business portion of rent ≈ $3,693 (plus business share of utilities, renters insurance, repairs). You’d use Form 8829 for the actual method. The actual method often gives a larger deduction here because of the high rent, but you must have records for expenses you allocate.
- Self‑employment tax: You will owe self‑employment (SE) tax on net Schedule C income (roughly 15.3% before the deduction). You can deduct half of your SE tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040.
- Student loan interest: Up to $2,500 of student loan interest is an above‑the‑line deduction (reported on Schedule 1), subject to income phaseouts. You listed $4,100 interest paid — you cannot deduct more than $2,500 and eligibility depends on your modified adjusted gross income (MAGI). If your MAGI is above the phaseout range, the deduction is reduced or eliminated.
- Charitable contributions: Your $1,400 cash gift to United Way is deductible if you itemize. You need receipts (you said you have one).
- Interest income: The $920 + $320 = $1,240 interest is taxable and reported on Form 1040.
- Standard employee deductions (mortgage interest, property taxes, SALT): You did not list mortgage interest or property taxes. Note SALT deduction is limited ($10,000 cap). Without significant mortgage interest, property tax, or large medical expenses, your itemized total may be low.

Should you itemize or take the standard deduction?
- For 2024, the standard deduction for married filing jointly is $29,200. (If you want, I can confirm the exact current-year figure from the IRS, but this is the 2024 published amount.)
- You would compare $29,200 to your potential itemized total (charitable $1,400 + state income tax withheld and/or property taxes from your W-2/paystubs and any other deductible items). With only $1,400 in charitable contributions and no mortgage interest or large property taxes listed, it is very likely the standard deduction will be larger, so you should take the standard deduction.
- Important: The home office deduction is separate from the decision to itemize. You can claim the home office (a business expense on Schedule C) even if you take the standard deduction on Form 1040.

Forms you’ll need and where things go
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040), if needed — for adjustments such as student loan interest deduction (up to $2,500).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report the $8,900 contracting income and business expenses (including home office deduction entered either directly or via Form 8829).
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home, IF you use the actual home‑office method. If you use the simplified method you do NOT file Form 8829; instead enter the simplified deduction on Schedule C (line for home office).
- Schedule SE — calculate self‑employment tax and the deductible half of SE tax.
- 1099-NEC/1099-MISC — if clients issued 1099s, attach or keep for records (you still report the income even if you don’t receive a 1099).
- 1099-INTs — interest statements from Discover and Fidelity (report on Form 1040).
- State return — Illinois (IL‑1040) for state income tax (report wages and other income). Illinois has no local Chicago city income tax.

Other practical notes and next steps
- Student loan interest: Collect Form 1098‑E from MOHELA (shows interest paid). If the interest you paid in the tax year was $4,100, you can only claim up to $2,500 (subject to MAGI phaseouts). If you’re over the income limit, the deduction may be reduced or unavailable.
- Home office: Decide simplified vs actual. Simplified is easier and requires less documentation ($500 here). Actual is likely larger because of the high rent — but requires documenting rent, utilities, insurance, repairs and completing Form 8829.
- Estimated taxes: Because you have self‑employment income, you may owe quarterly estimated taxes (Form 1040‑ES) to avoid penalties. Also plan for SE tax on your net contracting income (you’ll get to deduct half of that on Form 1040).
- Recordkeeping: keep receipts for business expenses, charitable contribution receipts, 1098‑E for student loan interest, 1099s, bank interest statements, and rent/utility bills for home office allocation.
- If you want a precise comparison of itemizing vs standard, I can run the numbers if you provide:
  - W‑2 federal and state tax withheld amounts;
  - any property tax or mortgage interest you paid (if applicable);
  - total utilities/insurance/repairs if you want to use the actual home‑office method;
  - exact student loan interest amount and confirmation of whether that $4,100 is interest paid in the tax year (and whether it’s reported on a 1098‑E).

Would you like me to:
- Estimate your federal tax (with a rough calculation) using the numbers you gave, showing whether itemizing would help; or
- Draft an email to a CPA with a summary of your situation and the questions to ask; or
- Prepare a checklist of documents to gather for filing?

Tell me which and I’ll prepare it.

