Thanks — this is enough to give practical, actionable guidance. I’ll cover what you can deduct, whether you’ll likely itemize or take the standard deduction, how to treat the home office, and which tax forms you’ll need (plus next steps and a few important caveats).

1) Summary of your situation (from what you gave)
- Filing status: Married filing jointly (Chicago, IL).  
- W-2 wages: $79,400 (Desert Sun Logistics).  
- Self-employment (IT contracting): $8,900 (3 clients).  
- Interest income: $920 (Discover) + $320 (Fidelity) = $1,240.  
- Home: renting, rent $2,400/month (annual $28,800); exclusive home office = 100 sq ft of 780 sq ft (≈12.82%).  
- Other items: $4,100 student loan interest (MOHELA), $1,400 charitable donation (Local United Way), $890 business/unreimbursed expenses (unclear if these are employee or self-employed expenses).

2) What you can deduct (and where)
- Self-employment business expenses (Schedule C): You can deduct ordinary and necessary expenses for your IT contracting business (equipment, supplies, internet, software, the business share of utilities, business mileage, etc.). If the $890 are bona fide business expenses for your contracting, they belong on Schedule C. If those $890 are unreimbursed employee expenses (from a W‑2 job), they are not deductible for 2018–2025 under current law.
- Home office (SELF‑EMPLOYED only): Because you have contracting income, you can claim a home office deduction for that business if the home office is used regularly and exclusively for the business and is your principal place of business. You have two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5 × 100 sq ft = $500.
  - Regular method: allocate a percentage of actual home expenses. Your allocation = 100/780 ≈ 12.82%. Applied to annual rent $28,800 that’s about $3,692 of rent allocable to the business (plus a share of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., if applicable). The regular method will likely give a larger deduction in your case because 12.82% of rent alone ≈ $3,692 > $500.
  - Important: the home office deduction for a self-employed business is limited to net profit from that business. You can’t use the home-office deduction to create or increase an overall business loss; any disallowed amount can generally be carried forward to future years when you have sufficient business income.
- Self-employment tax and related deduction: You’ll owe self-employment (SE) tax on net self-employment income (Schedule SE). You may deduct one-half of SE tax as an adjustment on Form 1040.
- Student loan interest: Up to $2,500 of student loan interest is an above-the-line deduction on Form 1040 (reported on Schedule 1), subject to income phaseouts. You reported $4,100 interest paid — the deductible limit is typically $2,500 and the amount phases out at higher incomes, so you’ll need to check whether your modified adjusted gross income (MAGI) puts you in the phaseout range; with your combined income it’s possible you’ll still get most or all of the deduction but confirm on your return or with a preparer.
- Charitable contributions: Your $1,400 gift is deductible if you itemize. If you take the standard deduction instead (very likely — see next section), you can’t deduct those gifts on your federal return (unless you itemize).
- Interest income: Report the $1,240 of interest on your 1040 (banks will issue 1099‑INT). If your total interest is small, you typically don’t need Schedule B (that’s required only if interest/dividends > $1,500 or for certain foreign account situations), but you still report the amounts on Form 1040.

3) Itemize vs. Standard deduction — what’s likely best for you
- Standard deduction for married filing jointly is substantial and typically higher than modest itemized deductions. Your only known itemized items are $1,400 charitable contributions (and possibly state/local taxes paid — you didn’t list property tax or state income tax beyond what’s withheld). With just $1,400 of charitable donations, you are very likely better off taking the standard deduction unless you also have significant mortgage interest, large property taxes, large medical expenses above 7.5% of AGI, or substantial other deductible items.
- Action: when preparing your return, compute both (itemized total vs. standard deduction) and pick the larger. For many couples in situations like yours, standard deduction is the result.

4) How the home office affects your taxes (example using your numbers)
- Home office percent ≈ 12.82%. Rent portion = $28,800 × 12.82% ≈ $3,692 (plus any allocable utilities, internet portion, renter’s insurance).
- If you claim the regular method, that amount reduces your Schedule C business income. Example rough math:
  - Gross self‑employment income: $8,900
  - Less home office rent portion (≈$3,692) and other business expenses (e.g., $890 if that’s business-related) → estimated net profit may be roughly $8,900 − $3,692 − $890 ≈ $4,318 (very approximate).
  - SE tax applies to ~92.35% of net profit; then half of SE tax is an above-the-line deduction on Form 1040.
- If the home office deduction (plus other business deductions) would exceed your contracting income for the year, the excess home-office deductions cannot be used to increase an overall tax loss for the year; disallowed amounts are carried forward to future years when you have business income.

5) Forms you’ll need (federal and state)
- Federal:
  - Form 1040 (individual income tax return).
  - Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments) — student loan interest adjustment, etc.
  - Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the $8,900 contracting income and business expenses.
  - Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax.
  - Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if you use the regular home office method (not required for the simplified method).
  - Form 8995 or 8995-A (Qualified Business Income deduction) — you may be eligible for the 20% QBI deduction on your contracting income; use the shorter Form 8995 if you qualify for the simplified calculation (most taxpayers with modest income do).
  - Form(s) 1099‑INT (interest) and Form W‑2 from your employer (these are informational but you report the amounts on 1040).
  - Possibly Schedule B only if interest/dividends exceed thresholds (not likely with $1,240 total).
  - Form 1040-ES (estimated tax vouchers) — if you expect to owe substantial tax during a year, use these to pay estimated quarterly taxes (self-employment income causes SE tax and income tax withholding shortfalls).
  - Form 4562 only if you are depreciating business assets (not usually needed just for a renter’s home office).
- State:
  - Illinois IL‑1040 (state return). Illinois has no local (Chicago) income tax. Be sure to include income and any state tax withholding or payments.

6) Practical next steps / checklist
- Gather documents: W‑2, any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC for contracting, 1099‑INTs, receipts for the $1,400 charity, proof of student loan interest (Form 1098‑E or lender statement), receipts/invoices for business expenses (the $890), and a copy of your lease and utility bills to support the home office allocation.
- Decide which home office method to use: calculate the regular method share of rent + utilities vs. simplified $5/sqft. Based on rent alone, the regular method looks better for you, but include any other allocable expenses in the calculation.
- Compute whether you need to make estimated tax payments (1040‑ES) to avoid penalties — if you expect to owe $1,000+ when you file, pay estimated quarterly.
- Consider QBI: the 20% Qualified Business Income deduction may reduce taxable income for your contracting income — run the numbers with your tax software or preparer.
- If you’re unsure about student loan interest phaseout or QBI complexity, or if you have other unlisted deductions (mortgage interest, property tax, big medical expenses), consult a CPA or tax preparer.

7) Caveats and recommendations
- I’ve given general guidance; tax law details (phaseout thresholds, standard deduction inflation adjustments, etc.) can change year-to-year. For precise dollar amounts (e.g., exact student‑loan interest phaseout thresholds for tax year 2024, the official standard deduction for 2024), check IRS publications for the tax year you’re filing or confirm with a tax professional.
- If any of the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” are from your W‑2 job, they are not deductible under current law. Only self‑employment expenses are deductible on Schedule C.
- If you want, I can:
  - run rough calculations with the exact numbers to estimate your federal tax liability and whether the home office regular method or simplified method is better, or
  - draft a checklist email you can send to your tax preparer with all the facts and documents needed.

Would you like me to:
- do a quick estimated-tax calculation now using the numbers you provided (I’ll show rough SE tax, effect of home office regular vs simplified), or
- draft a short email you can send to your tax preparer listing everything they’ll need?

