Thanks — this is enough to give a clear next-step plan and a likely outcome. Quick summary up front, then details and a few questions so I can refine estimates or prepare anything for your tax preparer.

Short answer / likely result
- For 2024 married filing jointly the standard deduction is likely larger than your known itemizable deductions, so you will almost certainly take the standard deduction (2024 MFJ standard deduction ≈ $29,200). 
- Your $8,900 IT contracting income should be reported on Schedule C; you’ll owe self-employment (SE) tax on net self-employment earnings and can deduct half of the SE tax as an adjustment to income.
- Because you have self-employment income and a dedicated, exclusive home office (100 sq ft of a 780 sq ft rental), you can take a home office deduction on Schedule C. Using the actual-expense share of rent gives a much larger deduction than the simplified method in your situation (approx $3,688 vs $500).
- Student loan interest: only up to $2,500 may be deductible in 2024 and the deduction phases out based on MAGI — also you can only deduct interest actually paid in tax year 2024 (and you should have a 1098‑E). If the $4,100 interest relates to 2016–2019 payments, those are not deductible on your 2024 return.
- Forms you will likely need: Form 1040, Schedule 1 (adjustments), Schedule C (profit/loss from business), Schedule SE (self-employment tax). If you itemize (unlikely) add Schedule A. Report interest on 1040 (and Schedule B only if interest > $1,500). State: IL-1040 for Illinois.

Details & calculations (using the figures you gave)
- Rent/year = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800.
- Home-office share = 100 sq ft / 780 sq ft = 12.82%.
  - Actual-expense home-office rent share = 12.82% × $28,800 ≈ $3,688.
  - Simplified method = $5 × 100 sq ft = $500.
  - Use the larger of the two if you want maximum deduction, but remember actual method requires documenting prorated utilities, rent, etc.
  - Home-office deduction is taken on Schedule C and cannot exceed your net profit from the business.
- Self-employment tax (approximate):
  - If net self-employment income is $8,900 with only the home-office deduction ($3,688) applied, net ≈ $5,212.
  - SE tax ≈ 15.3% × (92.35% of net earnings) ≈ $700–$800 (rough estimate; I calculated ≈ $736).
  - Half of SE tax is an above-the-line deduction on Schedule 1 (so reduces AGI).
- Interest income: $920 + $320 = $1,240 — reportable on Form 1040. (No Schedule B unless > $1,500.)
- Charitable gift: $1,400 — deductible only if itemizing. Given a likely standard deduction of ~$29,200, itemizing for $1,400 won’t help unless you have other sizable deductions (mortgage interest, big state taxes, large medical expenses, etc.). Note SALT cap $10,000 applies for state and local taxes if you do itemize.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses ($890) — not deductible for most employees under current law (TCJA suspension through 2025). If these were business expenses for your contracting work, they should be claimed on Schedule C instead.

Documents & forms to collect
- W-2 from Desert Sun Logistics.
- 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC or records showing $8,900 contracting income (if no 1099s, still report the income).
- Records/receipts for all business expenses (including the $890 — clarify whether that’s for the contract work).
- Proof of home office size (floor plan/photos) and a copy of your lease and rent paid (bank statements).
- 1098‑E for student loan interest paid in 2024 (if any). If loans were paid in previous years, those amounts are not deductible on the 2024 return.
- 1099‑INT forms from Discover and Fidelity (they will issue those for interest reported).
- Receipts for charitable donation ($1,400 receipt noted).

Questions I need to refine the recommendation or produce an estimate
1. Were the student loan interest payments of $4,100 made in 2024 or earlier (2016–2019 as your note suggests)? Did you receive a 1098‑E for 2024?
2. Is the $8,900 gross contracting amount or your net after expenses? (Are any of the $890 unreimbursed expenses related to this contracting work?)
3. Do you expect any other itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, significant state income tax paid, property tax, large medical expenses)?
4. Did any of the three contracting clients issue 1099‑NEC forms? Did you make any estimated tax payments for 2024?
5. Do you want a quick estimated federal tax calculation showing approximate tax owed/refund if you provide W-2 withholding and confirm answers to Q1–Q4?

Notes and cautions
- This is general guidance, not tax advice. Rules like the student loan interest phase-out depend on MAGI and filing status; exact numbers require running your full AGI calculation. For complex situations or if you want to minimize tax with planning (e.g., estimated tax payments, retirement contributions, timing of deductions), I recommend contacting a licensed CPA or enrolled agent.
- If you expect to owe tax because of the contracting income, consider making (or catching up on) estimated tax payments to avoid penalties.

If you want, I can:
- Run a rough tax estimate now if you give W-2 withholding and answer the 5 questions above.
- Draft an email for you to a tax preparer with all of these numbers and documents to collect.
- Prepare a one-page worksheet showing the calculations (home office actual vs simplified, SE tax estimate, effect on AGI).

Which of those would you like me to do next?

